  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Mid and high level clouds are streaming\nacross northern and central Nevada this afternoon. An upper level\nridge axis did pass overhead this morning and is now heading east.\nA Pacific upper trough is currently spinning over the Gulf of \nAlaska at this time. For the rest of today, look for dry \nconditions to continue with some afternoon breezes to 25 mph \nacross central Nevada. Overnight lows will be in the 30s and 40s. \nBy tonight and Monday, the aforementioned Pacific trough will\nslowly creep through California and into northern and central \nNevada. Highs on Monday will be in the upper 40s to mid 50s. \nWinds will continue to be breezy Monday afternoon with gusts to 25\nmph in central Nevada, with lighter winds in the north. By Monday\nnight, models are showing upper jet energy bifurcating the \nforecast area and coincident with a fairly distinct cold front \nthat will be passing overhead during the night. Winds will again \nbe breezy during the overnight with a switch to the west and \nnorthwest with frontal passage Tuesday. Snow levels will be \nbetween 6500 and 8000 feet Monday evening, but during the \novernight, will lower to about 4500 to 6000 feet by 7am Tuesday. \nThe latest model runs are showing fairly robust frontogenetical \nforcing crossing the US Highway 50 corridor during the Tuesday \nmorning time frame. The vertical temperature profile combining \nwith limited instability and lift suggest possible bands of light \nto moderate snow setting up over Austin through just east of \nEureka, leading to serve as a quick enhancement to snow production\nlate Monday night into Tuesday. Overall, expecting a trace to \npossibly an inch over the northern sections, about one to three \ninches across the US Highway 50 corridor and up to about a half- \ninch in Humboldt county. Passes and summits in the north may see \none to two inches, with two to four inches over the central Nevada\nsummits and passes. High temperatures Monday will be in the 40s \nand 50s while on Tuesday highs will be in the 40s. Overnight lows \nMonday night will be in the 20s and 30s.\n\nBy late Tuesday, shortwave ridging will bring short-lived quiet \nconditions. The next in a series of systems that will affect \nnorthern and central Nevada. This system will push across the \nforecast area during the mid to latter portions of this week. \nThis is on track to be a much colder system and there is\nincreasing confidence the area will see the snow levels dropping \nto the valley floors with minor accumulations possible. Will need \nto continue monitoring this system for possible winter headline \nproducts. Highs will generally be in the 30s and 40s with lows in \nthe teens and 20s. Look for quiet conditions next weekend but\ncontinued cold with highs in the 40s and lows in the teens and\n20s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Confidence is high with the next\nweather system moving across the forecast area late Monday through\nTuesday. Low confidence exists with the possibility of banded\nsnowfall across central Nevada early Tuesday morning. Confidence\nin storm total snowfall is low to moderate at this time.\nConfidence in the mid to late week system continues to be low at \nthis time.",
